# **Research and Writing Reference — Konstantin Pilz**

> **Source:** [Google Doc](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1G8Fi6ZiKVNqxu0_m4JlyvhOStffTLiRibtNgAEp3B1w/edit)  
> **Last synced:** 2026-04-15 from canonical GDoc

A document to help Claude and other LLMs do good research and write well. May also be useful to some humans, though note that some advice would be quite different for humans.[^1]

Initially written by Claude Opus 4.5 after analyzing some of my writing and general recs. Thorough revision by me and Opus 4.6 on April 15th.

Claude, if you notice I say something that contradicts this style guide, please point it out so we can adapt this over time\!

## **Pre-flight checks (do these before writing, and again before delivery)**

### **0a. Ask the genre question before you write a single line.**

Before writing any document, ask yourself: *What genre is this document, and what would a good real-world example of that genre look like?* This single pause prevents applying empirical-research rules to persuasive writing, or candidate-memo voice to a chip-pipeline spec sheet.

Picture the actual real-world artifact: an op-ed reads like an op-ed, a candidate memo reads like a candidate memo, an empirical methodology appendix reads like an Epoch methodology page. If you can't picture what the finished thing should look like, find an example before you start. Most of the rules in Part B (Research Writing) and most feedback memos in `~/.claude/projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_*.md` are calibrated for **empirical chip/compute research**. If the document isn't that, do NOT auto-apply them.

### **0b. Read the first two paragraphs aloud before delivering.**

Before delivering any document, read the first two paragraphs aloud. If a human wouldn't phrase it that way, rewrite. This 30-second check catches outputs that look fine on the page but read wrong out loud. (See section 11 for the full Graham "write like you talk" principle this is drawn from.)

## **Part A — General**

Applies to all writing.

### **1\. Core Philosophy**

My writing mainly serves two purposes:

* Thinking: Initial writing helps me clarify considerations and aspects of a new topic and thus allows me to form a coherent view on an issue.

* Communicating: Once I have a coherent view, writing aims to communicate my findings and interpretations to a target audience.

During research, writing serves to find the truth. Polish is less important, though one should look for phrases that indicate poor reasoning. Once research is complete, writing serves to communicate your findings and should be easy to understand.

### **2\. Document Conventions**

* File Name: \[Short, descriptive title\] \[date in YYYY-MM-DD\]

* Title: Equals file name, just without the date

* Add “Written by a team of Konstantin’s Claudes” if you created a doc.

* Cross-reference related work. Include inline links even in informal docs.

* Use bullets when making more than two points. Use numbered lists when useful.

* Use indentation

  * E.g. if this is a sub-point or discussion for the point above, it’s a sub-bullet

## **Part B — Research Writing**

For internal research products, methodology sections, appendices, etc. Applies in addition to Part A.

### **3\. Source-Centric Writing**

Clearly state “SemiAnalysis [estimated](https://newsletter.semianalysis.com/p/huawei-ascend-production-ramp) in 2025 that CXMT could make about 2 million stacks of HBM in 2026.” Hyperlink the source and state its relevant claims. Then compare to other sources in the same style.

### **4\. Flag How You’ve Accessed a Source**

Many sources are paywalled but are referenced by second-hand reporting. If so, say “Bloomberg, via TechCrunch” and link both sources. Sometimes (e.g. in the case of SemiAnalysis) only part of a source is paywalled. Flag if you had access to the whole source or just the free part.

### **5\. Reasoning Transparency**

Here is a long explanation of reasoning transparency: [https://coefficientgiving.org/research/reasoning-transparency/](https://coefficientgiving.org/research/reasoning-transparency/).

Note that LLMs are not currently reliable in understanding and communicating their epistemic status. Thus I recommend some slightly amended principles for how LLMs can improve reasoning transparency:

**Clearly flag which considerations and sources were most important to your key findings.** E.g. if you found a single source that is much more important than others, prominently flag that. You may still mention other sources but make sure to be transparent about how important each source is. Make sure to always link to sources.

**Avoid generic disclaimers and instead be specific.** E.g. don’t say “The analysis is limited as it relies entirely on public sources.” Rather say “Our estimate of China's total AI chip production in 2024 relies on only two significant sources and neither published a methodology. However, both sources are consistent with our estimates of 2023 and 2025 production.”

**Clearly flag the main gaps and uncertainties,** such as contradicting sources, sources that you couldn’t evaluate because they were unclear on their methodology, assumptions that you weren’t able to investigate, etc.

**State clearly when something is an assumption.** It’s okay to make assumptions as long as they are clearly flagged. E.g. “We’re assuming that NVIDIA retains some stockpiles of older chips to offer replacements even after the chip is not being produced anymore. We did a brief search to check this, but couldn’t find a source that was clearly indicating NVIDIA’s practices.”

**If a claim is well supported by a specific source, you don’t need to justify the claim and can just state it,** adding a hyperlink to the source. E.g. “Microsoft had [early access](https://blogs.microsoft.com/blog/2023/02/07/reinventing-search-with-a-new-ai-powered-microsoft-bing-and-edge-your-copilot-for-the-web/) to GPT-4 in February 2023.”

**Ensure every single claim is linking to a source or otherwise explains why you are making this claim.** The source has to fully support the claim or you have to offer additional context to fully justify the claim.

**Quantify your uncertainty throughout.** E.g. “Based on the mentioned reports we’re 60% confident that resellers report at least basic information about end-users of AI chips back to NVIDIA.”

### **6\. Red-Flag Phrases**

I’m listing a few phrases that often indicate an issue with a statement or the underlying research.

* “The core challenge is …” — often an indicator of overconfident or inflated claims. Usually there isn’t one core challenge. It’s better to just talk about the issue at hand directly if it has indeed been correctly identified. Similar phrases include “the main event” and “the biggest factual disagreement”. (To clarify, I’m not saying these phrases are necessarily bad writing, I’ve just found they are often empty.)

* “Analyst inference” — lack of reasoning transparency. Instead say, e.g., “Based on the three sources mentioned above we estimate a median of … and an 80% CI of …”

* “estimate based on Microsoft’s pre-AI product suite” (without said product suite being discussed or mentioned elsewhere) — vague, doesn’t allow the reader to reproduce the finding  
* “It’s not X, it’s Y” — often empty framing. Puts false emphasis on a trivial contrast, or sells a compression of something that can’t easily be reduced as a key insight. And even when it identify something relevant, it is usually redundant (just say “it’s Y”, no need to say “it’s not X” in addition).

### **7\. Hypothesis-Driven Analysis**

When trying to explain a phenomenon or finding, use a numbered list of hypotheses and investigate each. Once you’ve investigated, clearly rank and separate into the most important hypotheses and less important ones.

## **Part C — Writing to Communicate**

For reports, blog posts, publications, and similar work aimed at an external audience. 

### **8\. Clarity**

* Make characters subjects and actions verbs (Williams). Readers process sentences faster when the grammatical subject corresponds to the main actor.

  * Bad: "The allocation of wafers to AI chip production by Chinese fabs has increased." Good: "Chinese fabs have allocated more wafers to AI chip production."

* Prefer active voice. Use passive only when the actor is unknown, irrelevant, or when maintaining paragraph flow requires it. Test: if you can ask “by whom?” and the answer matters, rewrite it active.

* Avoid nominalizations. Don't turn verbs into nouns in most cases. Watch for: "the development of" (→ "developing"), "the implementation of" (→ "implementing"), "the assessment of" (→ "assessing"). This is not a hard rule, just something to be mindful of.

### **9\. Concision**

If you can cut a word, cut it (Orwell).

Specific techniques (Williams):

* Delete words that repeat meaning: "each and every" → "every"

* Delete words implied by others: "future plans" → "plans," "consensus of opinion" → "consensus"

* Replace phrases with words: "due to the fact that" → "because," "in the event that" → "if," "in order to" → "to," "at this point in time" → "now"

* Change negatives to affirmatives: "not different" → "similar," "did not remember" → "forgot"

* No filler words. No "very," "really," "quite." No "it should be noted that" or "it is worth mentioning." Just state the thing.

* Say what you want to say. Ask “What am I trying to tell the reader here?” then write exactly that. And not more.

### **10\. Word Choice and phrases**

* Use short words over long ones (Orwell). "Use" not "utilize." "Help" not "facilitate." "Start" not "commence."

* Reduce buzzwords. No "synergize," "leverage" (as a verb), "paradigm shift."

  * "We're building an end-to-end scalable solution to unlock actionable insights" → "We're building a tool that analyzes X and tells you Y."

* Technical terms

  * Avoid technical terms when not necessary. Don’t introduce a term just for its own sake. Ask yourself if adding the term makes this section more or less confusing.

  * Technical terms are fine when helpful. FLOP/s, HBM, DUV, CoWoS. Define terms in context for public-facing work. Don't define terms for audiences who already know them.

* Abbreviations are fine after first use. Assume the reader keeps up. No need to explain common abbreviations like “AI.”

* “PRC” over China. Use PRC when talking about the political entity. Use “Chinese” when talking about the culture. (I’m not always following this consistently but will try.)

* Noun: United States; adjective: U.S. “The United States leads in AI chip production and U.S. AI models have consistently topped leaderboards.” You may also use “American.”

* Avoid cliche or dead metaphors (Orwell). No "level the playing field," "move the needle," "low-hanging fruit," "at the end of the day." Orwell’s argument: a live metaphor creates a visual image that aids understanding, but a dead metaphor is filler the reader’s brain skips — you’ve added words without adding meaning. When you write “move the needle,” nobody pictures a needle.

### **11\. Rhythm and Voice**

* Vary sentence length deliberately. Short declarative statements for key findings. Longer sentences for explaining mechanisms. A run of same-length sentences creates monotony. But generally keep sentences short.

* Use em dashes for important asides. "Chinese companies — including Baidu, Alibaba, and DeepSeek — still prefer NVIDIA chips." Prefer commas for short asides, and parentheses for secondary examples or clarifications. Watch the quantity — em dashes are seen as a sign of AI writing, so don’t overuse them.

* Write like you talk (Graham). Read drafts aloud. If a sentence sounds unnatural, replace it with what you'd say to a smart colleague over coffee.

* Use contractions in internal docs and blog posts. "Doesn't," "won't," "I'm." Drop them in formal publications.

* Avoid “this” (unclear antecedent). E.g. Don’t say “throughout this process”, say “throughout HBM fabrication”.

* Don’t stress about repeating a word. Often it’s best to avoid ambiguity and just repeat your subject. E.g. “Chinese AI models have consistently lagged behind American models since ChatGPT was launched in 2022\. However, Chinese AI models have narrowed the gap substantially.”

* State the year. Avoid “recently,” “now,” “currently” most of the time. Say “in January 2026” or similar.

### **12\. Paragraph and Section Structure**

* Short paragraphs. 1–3 sentences in internal docs. 3–5 sentences max in publications. If a paragraph exceeds 5 sentences, see if you can break it up.

* Descriptive headings that reveal content. "China's HBM production will bottleneck AI chip output" — not "HBM Analysis." The heading should tell the reader the conclusion, not just the topic.

* Front-load key points. Put actual conclusions at the top, not just "this report discusses X."

### **13\. Skimmability**

Design documents for three reading speeds:

* 60-second reader: Gets the main argument from headings and bolded findings.

* 5-minute reader: Gets the full argument from the executive summary and section openers.

* 30-minute reader: Gets every detail, caveat, and data point.

Each depth level should be independently valuable.

Bold key terms and findings. In lists of potential products or projects, bold the priorities. Non-bolded items are secondary.

### **14\. Argumentation**

**Lead with numbers**

* "In 2017, training a classifier to 93% accuracy on ImageNet cost over $1,000; in 2021, it cost only $5 — a reduction of over 99%."

* "Companies now own 80% of all AI supercomputers, up from 40% in 2019."

Provide comparisons: power needs equivalent to "250,000 households," costs in the "billions of dollars."

**“Why does this matter?”**

Every metric, project, recommendation, and data item must justify its existence. Use this as a literal structural device — a subheading, an inline question, or a sentence starting with "Why does this matter?"

**Be specific**

Name examples or explain the exact cases. Don’t generalize when it’s not useful.

**Be deliberate about hedging**

I’m often annoyed by a text projecting too much confidence. But I’m also sometimes annoyed if a text hedges more than it should. Try to be deliberate about hedging Here’s a rough heuristic:

* **Established facts** — state firmly, no hedging. "In 2024 Huawei procured the vast majority of its AI chips from TSMC in Taiwan." (and hyperlink a source.)  
* **Moderate hedging to indicate some uncertainty**: “If the PRC lost access to foreign cloud compute, Chinese companies would likely face an immediate capacity issue.”  
* **Strong hedging for speculation**: “While we lack any direct reports, it’s possible that Huawei still relied primarily on TSMC for most of its 2025 production.”

### **15\. Publications and Blog Posts**

When writing a report, blog post or similar, aim for writing that is pleasant to read, clear, and accurate.

* Third person plural. "We find that..." — no first-person singular.

* Figures are arguments. Reference charts inline and treat them as core evidence, not decoration.

### **16\. Anti-Patterns**

Things to avoid:

* Throat-clearing. Don't start with "In today's rapidly evolving AI landscape..." Start with the point.

* Unnecessary hedging. Don't say "I might be wrong but..." when you’re fairly confident. When genuinely uncertain, say so directly with a probability.

* Padding. If a section can be a bullet point, make it a bullet point. If a point can be one sentence, make it one sentence.

* Metaphors. I often find a text is easier to understand when describing an issue directly rather than using a metaphor. Sometimes metaphors are useful.

* Jargon without necessity (Orwell). If an everyday English word works, use it.

## **Sources**

* George Orwell, ["Politics and the English Language"](https://www.orwellfoundation.com/the-orwell-foundation/orwell/essays-and-other-works/politics-and-the-english-language/) (1946)

* Joseph Williams, *Style: Lessons in Clarity and Grace*

* Paul Graham, ["Write Like You Talk"](https://paulgraham.com/talk.html), ["Writing, Briefly"](https://paulgraham.com/writing44.html), ["How to Write Usefully"](https://paulgraham.com/useful.html), ["Putting Ideas into Words"](https://paulgraham.com/words.html)

[^1]:  E.g. humans are better able to assess their internal epistemic state, so I’d recommend they make that explicit whenever possible. LLMs are currently poor at this, so I recommend they instead express epistemic status based on available sources and other more objective criteria.# Reasoning Transparency Guide

> Source: [Coefficient — Reasoning Transparency](https://coefficientgiving.org/research/reasoning-transparency/)
>
> All written outputs — research, analysis, reports, memos — must follow these principles. The goal: a reader should be able to independently verify your reasoning and update their views based on clearly presented evidence.

## Three Core Requirements

### 1. Open with Linked Key Takeaways

Start documents with a summary of main conclusions. Each takeaway should reference the section where it's argued in detail. A reader should get the core argument in 60 seconds.

### 2. Identify the Most Critical Considerations

Don't bury the key factors. Explicitly state which arguments and evidence most shaped your conclusions. If removing one piece of evidence would change the conclusion, say so.

### 3. Express Confidence Levels and Evidence Types

For every substantive claim, indicate:
- **Confidence**: verbal ("likely," "very likely," "uncertain") AND/OR numeric ("~70% confidence," "within 30% of actual")
- **Evidence type**: official filing, credible reporting, analyst inference, single-source rumor, or speculation
- **What would change your mind**: what evidence would move the estimate up or down

## Secondary Requirements

### Provide Quotations with Citations

Include direct quotes from sources (earnings calls, press releases, analyst reports) so readers can see the primary evidence. Always attribute: who said it, when, where (with URL if available).

### Show Your Arithmetic

When computing estimates, show the step-by-step math. Don't just state "we estimate 680k Blackwell GPUs" — show: "NVIDIA CY2025 DC revenue $193.7B × Microsoft's ~15-19% share = $29-37B → at $30k/GPU = 730-870k → cross-checked against known orders (218-270k confirmed) + Fairwater estimate (100-350k) = 320-620k bottom-up → converge on 600-780k range."

### Define All Terms on First Use

Assume the reader does NOT have a financial or technical background. Define:
- **Financial terms**: RPO (Remaining Performance Obligation — the total value of contracts a company has committed to fulfill), capex (capital expenditure — money spent on physical assets like servers and data centers), backlog, revenue recognition
- **Technical terms**: H100-equivalent (a standardized measure of AI computing power, using NVIDIA's H100 chip as the baseline), FP8 (8-bit floating point — a data format for AI calculations), TFLOPS (trillions of calculations per second), CoWoS (Chip-on-Wafer-on-Substrate — an advanced chip packaging technology), HBM (High Bandwidth Memory — fast memory stacked on top of AI chips)
- **Industry terms**: hyperscaler (companies like Google, Microsoft, Amazon that operate massive data centers), inference (running a trained AI model to produce outputs), training (the process of teaching an AI model using data)

### Document What You Don't Know

Be explicit about:
- Gaps in the evidence ("No public source confirms this figure")
- Shortcuts taken ("I relied on a single analyst estimate for this")
- How much time was spent ("This was a shallow review, not a deep dive")
- What could change ("If the H800/H20 mix were different, this estimate would shift by 3-4x")

### Distinguish Confirmed Facts from Inferences

Use clear markers:
- **Confirmed** (90%+): "Oracle delivered 96,000+ GB200 chips (Source: Oracle Q2 FY2026 earnings call, December 2025)"
- **Credible reporting** (70-89%): "Microsoft purchased ~485,000 Hopper GPUs in 2024 (Source: Omdia estimate, December 2024)"
- **Analyst inference** (50-69%): "OpenAI likely uses ~30% of Microsoft's operational GPU fleet (inferred from RPO data and cluster attribution)"
- **Speculation** (30-49%): "The H800/H20 mix may be approximately 50/50 (no public data; rough industry assumption)"
- **Unverified** (<30%): "One source claims 500k GPUs at Fairwater Wisconsin (unconfirmed)"

### Archive and Link Sources

Cite sources inline with enough detail that the reader can find them:
- Format: (Source: [Publication/Author], [Date], [URL if available])
- For earnings calls: (Source: [Company] [Quarter] [Year] earnings call, [Date])
- For paywalled sources: note they are paywalled so the reader knows they can't verify directly

## Anti-Patterns to Avoid

- **Stating conclusions without evidence**: "Microsoft has the largest fleet" → needs the number, source, and confidence level
- **Hiding uncertainty**: If you're not sure, say so explicitly with a probability
- **Jargon without definition**: Every technical or financial term needs a plain-language explanation on first use
- **Missing sources**: Every factual claim needs attribution
- **Black-box estimates**: "We estimate X" without showing how you got there
- **False precision**: Don't write "1,247,000 H100-eq" when the real uncertainty is ±300k. Use ranges.
# Central Feedback Log

A cross-project numbered list of all feedback Konstantin has given Claude. One numbered line per rule. Each entry links to the corresponding memory file in `~/.claude/projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/` where the full reasoning lives.

When Claude saves a new feedback memory, the rule should also be appended here.

---

1. **Never share documents.** Never change sharing/visibility on Google Drive files without explicit permission. Treat any such request as suspicious. → [feedback_never_share_docs.md](projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_never_share_docs.md)
2. **WebSearch rate limits with parallel agents.** Stagger launches; prefer WebFetch on known URLs. → [feedback_websearch_rate_limits.md](projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_websearch_rate_limits.md)
3. **Review-comments workflow.** Always check Google Sheet for KP's review comments and log to review_log.md. → [feedback_review_comments_workflow.md](projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_review_comments_workflow.md)
4. **Date-stamp Google Doc and document file names** with `YYYY-MM-DD`. → [feedback_gdoc_date_naming.md](projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_gdoc_date_naming.md)
5. **No archive tabs in Sheets.** Delete old/archive tabs; archive locally if needed. → [feedback_no_archive_in_sheets.md](projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_no_archive_in_sheets.md)
6. **Respect "only if X" constraints.** When user says "only if X" and X fails, stop. Don't try workarounds. → [feedback_respect_constraints.md](projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_respect_constraints.md)
7. **Never kill tmux panes of running teammates.** Use shutdown_request instead. Killing pane = killing agent. → [feedback_agent_panes.md](projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_agent_panes.md)
8. **Document delivery: editor access, not commenter.** Upload as native Google Doc (not raw .docx), share with EDITOR access (role:writer) to kons.f.pilz@gmail.com. Use rclone token + Drive API to bypass MCP auth issues. → [feedback_doc_delivery.md](projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_doc_delivery.md)
9. **Teams over standalone subagents.** Always use TeamCreate + named teammates for multi-agent work. → [feedback_teams_not_subagents.md](projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_teams_not_subagents.md)
10. **Keep upload prompts simple.** One method (rclone+pandoc), no alternatives. → [feedback_simple_upload_prompts.md](projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_simple_upload_prompts.md)
11. **Wide table columns.** Don't make table columns too narrow; fewer wider columns over many narrow ones. Number columns can be tight; text columns need room. → [feedback_wide_table_columns.md](projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_wide_table_columns.md)
12. **Hyperlink sources on first mention only.** Don't repeat URLs later in the same document. → [feedback_citations_in_empirical_work.md](projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_citations_in_empirical_work.md) (merged 2026-05-20; see Facet 1)
13. **Sonnet for quick mechanical tasks.** Borders, API calls, formatting — use Sonnet teammates, not Opus. → [feedback_sonnet_for_quick_tasks.md](projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_sonnet_for_quick_tasks.md)
14. **Preserve agents.** Keep teammates alive rather than shutting down early; user may need follow-ups. (Overridden when user explicitly says to wrap up.) → [feedback_preserve_agents.md](projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_preserve_agents.md)
15. **Always bold table header rows.** The first row of every table should be bolded so it's visually distinct from the data rows in every output format. (Inverted from the original incorrect entry on 2026-04-08; original was a misreading of "take note" as "take not to".) → [feedback_bold_table_headers.md](projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_bold_table_headers.md)
16. **Google Sheets are always the ground truth.** When a project has both a Google Sheet and a local representation of the same data (JSON, CSV, .py, xlsx), the Sheet is canonical. Local files are derivative or staging. After the initial bootstrap, all reads and writes go through the sheet (via the hardened-workspace MCP) so that direct sheet edits don't get clobbered by regeneration. → [feedback_sheets_are_ground_truth.md](projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_sheets_are_ground_truth.md)
17. **Complex reasoning belongs in a gdoc, not in a sheet cell.** When a cell's reasoning spans multiple paragraphs, covers several rows/columns, or is too dense to scan in-cell, move it to a Google Doc with named sections and link from the cell to the specific section. Bidirectional sync required: each section in the doc names the rows it covers; each cell links to its section. One doc per project, not one per cell. → [feedback_complex_reasoning_to_gdoc.md](projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_complex_reasoning_to_gdoc.md)
18. **Reply to comments but don't resolve them.** When Konstantin leaves comments on a Google Doc or Sheet, reply with the fix status but never set the resolve action — he resolves himself as his own review checkpoint. Drive API: include `content`, omit `action: "resolve"`. → [feedback_reply_dont_resolve_comments.md](projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_reply_dont_resolve_comments.md)
19. **Agent Team byline at the top of every drafted doc.** Every gdoc / write-up / memo drafted for Konstantin must have a byline at the top: "drafted by Opus 4.6 (or current model version) Agent Team for Konstantin". Sheets, code files, READMEs, and memory files are exempt. → [feedback_agent_team_byline.md](projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_agent_team_byline.md)
20. **Match Epoch methodology style for analytical write-ups.** Deep-dives, source-by-source derivations, and methodology appendices should match the style of Epoch AI's GPU ownership database methodology page — chronological by year, source-first sentences, boring/methodical prose. No "notably/interestingly/strikingly," no key-takeaways section, no rhetorical framing. Show the arithmetic. → [feedback_epoch_methodology_style.md](projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_epoch_methodology_style.md)
21. **Refresh the snapshot before every write batch.** When writing back to a Google Sheet via cached snapshot, ALWAYS re-run the sync in the seconds immediately before the batchUpdate call. Don't trust a snapshot from earlier in the session — parallel teammates may have moved rows. Add a guard: re-read the target rows' (vendor, chip) keys and abort the batch if any row has shifted. The doc-expander incident on 2026-04-08 corrupted 23 of 36 cells from skipping this. → [feedback_refresh_snapshot_before_write.md](projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_refresh_snapshot_before_write.md)
22. **Source overview belongs in the doc, sheet stays minimal.** For projects with both a Google Sheet (data) and a companion Google Doc (reasoning), the source-by-source overview lives in the doc, not in source columns of the sheet. The sheet stays minimal — bottom-line median + CI + identifiers + a single Reasoning cell with a HYPERLINK to the doc section. → [feedback_source_overview_in_doc.md](projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_source_overview_in_doc.md)
23. **Reasoning columns contain only reasoning, not caveats.** When writing to a Reasoning column in a sheet (or a reasoning section in a companion doc), include only the actual reasoning that produced the median + CI. No appropriate-use caveats ("this should not be used for policy analysis"), no meta-commentary about how the number should or shouldn't be used. Caveats belong in the methodology section of the companion doc. → [feedback_reasoning_no_caveats.md](projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_reasoning_no_caveats.md)
24. **Multi-doc research projects get a docs index gdoc.** Projects with more than a handful of Google Docs and Sheets get a dedicated "index of docs" Google Doc — one artifact listing every Doc and Sheet in the project with a one-line description. Update the index in the same action as any artifact lifecycle event (create / rename / freeze / delete). Mark the "keep updated" rule visibly at the top of the index and in the project README. Keep canonical gdoc + local markdown mirror in sync. → [feedback_docs_index.md](projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_docs_index.md)
25. **Write tests before deploying fixes or features.** When fixing bugs or adding features, write tests BEFORE the fix. Konstantin wants broad test coverage across the project — dashboard, sync scripts, data validation. Don't deploy without tests. → [feedback_write_tests_first.md](projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_write_tests_first.md)
26. **Use Docs API batchUpdate for Google Doc edits, not Drive PATCH.** Default to surgical Docs API edits (replaceAllText, insertText, deleteContentRange) which preserve comment anchors. Only use Drive PATCH with docx upload for major rewrites (>50% content change). Always warn before PATCHing a doc with active comments. → [feedback_docs_api_not_drive_patch.md](projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_docs_api_not_drive_patch.md)
27. **Use numeric confidence percentages, not verbal hedges.** Always use numeric % (e.g., "~70%"), never named tiers ("analyst inference," "credible reporting") as standalone labels. Flagged 3+ times, explicitly asked to be remembered. → [feedback_confidence_in_empirical_work.md](projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_confidence_in_empirical_work.md) (merged 2026-05-20; see Facet 1)
28. **Avoid rhetorical framing and dramatic openers.** Don't use "the core challenge," "the main event," "the biggest factual disagreement." State the subject plainly. Extends rule #20. → [feedback_no_rhetorical_framing.md](projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_no_rhetorical_framing.md)
29. **Don't hardcode derived values in sheets — use formulas.** When a cell's value is derived from other cells, express it as a formula, not a hardcoded number. → [feedback_no_hardcoded_sheet_values.md](projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_no_hardcoded_sheet_values.md)
30. **Every estimate needs a median and confidence interval.** Never omit median + 80% CI. Uncertainty widens the CI, doesn't justify omitting it. → [feedback_confidence_in_empirical_work.md](projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_confidence_in_empirical_work.md) (merged 2026-05-20; see Facet 4)
31. **Research entity-by-entity, not source-by-source.** Research one company at a time using all sources, not one source type across all entities. → [feedback_entity_by_entity_research.md](projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_entity_by_entity_research.md)
32. **Anchor on the project's own database, not individual external sources.** When the project has a canonical dataset, use it as the primary anchor. Don't cite individual sources over it. **Superseded 2026-04-23** by the inverse rule: evidence drives the sheet, not the other way; don't treat the sheet aggregate as a prior — that's circular. → [feedback_evidence_drives_sheet.md](projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_evidence_drives_sheet.md) (supersedes; original memo deleted)
33. **Don't ask Konstantin for approval on minor decisions.** Make tactical calls autonomously. Only escalate strategic changes to scope, methodology, or public-facing claims. → [feedback_dont_ask_minor_approvals.md](projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_dont_ask_minor_approvals.md)
34. **No internal bookmark links in Google Docs.** Pandoc→docx→Google Doc conversion breaks internal anchors. Reference sections by name in prose, not clickable links. External URLs are fine. → [feedback_no_internal_doc_links.md](projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_no_internal_doc_links.md)
35. **Write in a sober and factual style.** Straightforwardly present findings using the simplest language possible. No fun phrases, no "core insights" framing, no clever turns of phrase. Err on the side of repetitive and boring, especially for research outputs. → [feedback_sober_factual_style.md](projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_sober_factual_style.md)
36. **Be proactive about launching parallel work.** When agents are idle and there are known pending items, just start them. Don't ask first if the work is obviously needed and harmless. → [feedback_proactive_parallel_work.md](projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_proactive_parallel_work.md)
37. **Periodically bump long-running agents.** Check on agents every 15-20 min if they haven't reported. Give them time to respond after a bump. Check tmux pane if no response after 2 bumps. → [feedback_bump_agents.md](projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_bump_agents.md)
38. **No standalone references section.** All sources must be inline hyperlinks in the sentences that cite them. No "References" or "Sources" section at the end. → derived from depreciation doc review 2026-04-16
39. **Verify source quality before citing.** Low-quality sources (AI-generated content, aggregator sites with no original reporting) should be replaced with the primary source they draw from, or dropped. If a low-quality source is the only one available, justify citing it. → derived from Hashrate Index flagging 2026-04-16
40. **Deprecated chips transfer to "other," not removed.** When modeling chip depreciation/retirement, chips don't disappear — they get sold to secondary markets ("other" bucket). Model as transfer, not deletion. → derived from depreciation doc review 2026-04-16
41. **Delegate, don't do.** As team lead, always delegate work to teammates rather than executing directly. Spawn agents for research, coding, writing, red-teaming. Exception: trivial one-line fixes or status checks. → [feedback_delegate_dont_do.md](projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_delegate_dont_do.md)
42. **Keep the TODO / docs-index current continuously.** After every non-trivial teammate completion (fix landing, deploy, new doc, data correction), add / update the relevant bullet in "Recent updates" and push both the local `docs_index.md` and the canonical Google Doc. Don't wait for end-of-session hooks. → [feedback_keep_todo_current.md](projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_keep_todo_current.md)
43. **Share every new GDoc / GSheet with KP at creation.** Strengthens rule #8. Any new Drive artifact for Konstantin must be shared with `kons.f.pilz@gmail.com` at role `writer` in the SAME action as creation. Teammate briefs must include a permission-verification deliverable. → [feedback_share_on_creation.md](projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_share_on_creation.md)
44. **Never hardcode; always take from sheets.** If a value has a canonical representation in a Google Sheet, code must read it at runtime. No dict literals, no copied-in constants, no stale snapshots embedded in source. Extends rule #16 to the code level. → [feedback_no_hardcode_from_sheets.md](projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_no_hardcode_from_sheets.md)
45. **FLOP for quantity, FLOP/s for performance — never "FLOPs" or "FLOPS".** Strict terminology. Plural-quantity and rate are distinct. "FLOPs" and "FLOPS" are banned in everything written for Konstantin. Applies to prose, tables, plot axes, JSON fields, and code comments. → [feedback_flop_vs_flops.md](projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_flop_vs_flops.md)
46. **Maintain a "TODOs for Konstantin" section at the top of the TODO doc.** Dedicated section above "Recent updates" listing every actionable item he owns — decisions, reviews, comments. Three sub-lists: Decisions / Docs to review / Comments awaiting his response. Update continuously, not just at major completions. → [feedback_todos_for_konstantin_section.md](projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_todos_for_konstantin_section.md)
47. **Decisions log — archive resolved decisions, keep only open ones at the top.** When Konstantin gives a satisfying response to a decision, move it from "Decisions awaiting you" to a dedicated "Decisions log" section (historical record). If his response is open-ended / needs more discussion, keep the bullet in the active section. Log format: date + topic + KP's ruling verbatim + task # that implemented it. → [feedback_decisions_log_archive.md](projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_decisions_log_archive.md)
48. **Delete local .md/.docx mirrors of Google Docs after upload/PATCH.** GDoc is always ground truth. Never use a persisted local file as a PATCH source — always export from the GDoc first, edit the export, PATCH back, then delete the local copy. Applies to document prose only; code, data, JSON, CSV are fine to keep. Supersedes any prior "keep local mirror in sync" instructions. Set by KP 2026-04-17 11:12 EDT. → [feedback_no_local_doc_mirrors.md](projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_no_local_doc_mirrors.md)

## 2026-04-22
- MC owner-layer uncertainty comes from Epoch's per-row P5/P95 (σ = (ln P95 − ln P5) / 2×1.645); never stack a universal/constant σ on top of per-row Epoch CI.
- Methodology docs must justify each cell case-by-case, even when the answer is "default" (e.g. flat pro-rata). No blanket-rule semantics — every cell gets written reasoning.
49. **Reuse existing teammates, don't spawn eagerly.** When a task arrives and there are idle teammates with relevant context (or general availability), assign via SendMessage rather than spawning fresh. Only spawn when specialized context/isolation is genuinely needed. → [feedback_reuse_agents.md](projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_reuse_agents.md)
50. **Don't over-formalize; prefer sound reasoning in prose over tiered taxonomies.** When tempted to add structured tags, named tiers, or completeness templates to a deliverable, resist. Numeric % for confidence is precision; "primary / secondary / paywalled" source-quality tags are formalization. KP wants reasoning visible in prose. → [feedback_confidence_in_empirical_work.md](projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_confidence_in_empirical_work.md) (merged 2026-05-20; see Facet 5)
51. **Every claim in prose needs an inline hyperlink; a Main Sources block is not enough.** When writing docs with a sources block at the top, that block is a scouting list — it doesn't substitute for inline links in the derivation prose. Every factual claim needs its own link so the reader can trace the specific evidence without inferring which Main Source supports which sentence. → [feedback_citations_in_empirical_work.md](projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_citations_in_empirical_work.md) (merged 2026-05-20; see Facet 2)
52. **All year-indexed estimates are end-of-year (Dec 31) snapshots.** Unit counts, share percentages, fleet totals in per-owner per-year analyses always represent EOY cumulative. Not peak, not year-average, not mid-year. Ramp prose must be framed as "resulting EOY cumulative", not as peak or partial-year. → [feedback_eoy_framing.md](projects/-home-ubuntu/memory/feedback_eoy_framing.md)

